$DIR/main.p:59:10: cannot keep the closure pickStore(alloc.New[W](), &p, one) returns past this iteration of the loop: it is stored in keep, declared outside the loop (main.p:57)
    A function that returns a closure builds it in a region its caller provides
    (spec section 11.5). The region of a call in a loop is part of this frame,
    and the call uses the same region on every iteration, so the closure
    pickStore(alloc.New[W](), &p, one) returns on one iteration is overwritten
    on the next. Keeping it past the iteration, in keep, declared outside the
    loop (main.p:57), would leave it changing under you. Here the closure is
    passed back to pickStore, so each one would hold the one the call made
    before, in the region the call is about to refill: the chain grows with
    every iteration, and no region of fixed size can hold it.
    Compose a fixed number of closures, or keep what each step needs in a slice
    and return one closure that applies every step.
    Go allocates each closure on the heap. Plutonium builds it in the caller's
    frame, which has room for one closure per call site.
    (see "Closures and their regions" in the Plutonium spec, section 11.5)
    (run "pluto explain E3101" for a longer explanation)
